---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/expert/quantum-chemistry">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This expert lesson is where the rules you have been applying since <em>Chemical bonding</em>
    (intermediate) and <em>Thermodynamics and enthalpy</em> (intermediate) finally get derived
    rather than memorised. <em>Spectroscopy and structure</em> (advanced) taught you to
    interrogate molecules with light and magnets — here you learn why the energy ladder those
    techniques climb exists at all — and <em>Coordination chemistry</em> (advanced) showed you
    the transition metals that break the group patterns; quantum chemistry explains both.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state what the Schrödinger equation does and why its solutions — orbitals — replace the Bohr picture of electron paths;</li>
    <li>assign the four quantum numbers and use the Pauli exclusion principle to explain why shells hold 2, 8, 18 electrons;</li>
    <li>run the Born–Haber ledger for an ionic compound and show that electron transfer is electrostatic bookkeeping, not shells "wanting" anything;</li>
    <li>explain covalent bonding as orbital overlap, and read bond order, hybridisation and VSEPR geometry as consequences of it;</li>
    <li>connect structure decisions (diamond versus graphite, metal versus salt) to the bonding regime that produces them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum chemistry is where chemistry's rules are finally derived instead of memorised.
    Why elements fall into families, why shells hold 2, 8, 8; why carbon makes four bonds
    in a tetrahedron; why sodium hands an electron to chlorine but won't share with itself
    — all of it falls out of <strong>solving the Schrödinger equation for electrons around
    nuclei</strong>. The exact solutions exist only for hydrogen; the rest of the field is
    systematic approximation, now supercharged by computation. The school topic list —
    elements and mixtures, atomic structure, isotopes, ionic and covalent bonding, giant
    structures, metals — becomes, in this light, the ordered consequences of one wave
    equation, each topic a different regime of the same solution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from wave equation to periodic table — the logical chain" widget="equationStep" config={orbitalCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five panels are one logical chain, not five facts.
    Start at the wave equation and watch each step consume the previous one: the equation's
    standing-wave solutions force quantum numbers on you; the four quantum numbers plus Pauli
    force the filling order; the filling order builds the periodic table; and "bonds are orbital
    overlaps" extends the same solutions from one atom to two. If you can say why each step
    follows from the last, you hold the whole lesson in one sentence.
  </p>

  <h2>Elements, compounds and mixtures: identity from the spectrum</h2>
  <p>
    The school distinction (2.1) — elements cannot be decomposed, compounds have fixed
    ratios, mixtures are just neighbours — acquires a physical basis: each element has a
    unique line spectrum, because each nucleus produces a unique set of quantised electron
    levels, and light absorption or emission reveals the fingerprint. Helium was found in
    the Sun's spectrum (1868) 27 years before it was isolated on Earth; sodium's twin
    yellow lines identify it at parts-per-billion in streetlight spectra; astronomers read
    the composition of stars they will never touch. Compounds, by contrast, are new
    quantum objects: NaCl is not sodium-plus-chlorine, it is a lattice whose levels belong
    to neither atom — which is why table salt is neither a toxic metal nor a poisonous
    gas. The mixture/compound line is exactly the line where new energy levels appear, and
    spectroscopy sees it directly.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the gap — found in the sky before the lab" widget="estimate" config={qcHeliumEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> identity is spectral — every element's quantised levels are a
    fingerprint, and forming a compound rewrites those levels entirely. The next section opens
    the filing cabinet those levels live in: the periodic table as the wave equation's own
    organisation.
  </p>

  <h2>Atomic structure and the Periodic Table: the addresses of electrons</h2>
  <p>
    The periodic table (2.2) is the wave equation's greatest hit. Each electron gets four
    quantum numbers: n (energy and size), ℓ (shape — s spheres, p dumbbells, d cloverleaves),
    mℓ (orientation), and m<sub>s</sub> (spin up or down). The Pauli exclusion principle —
    no two electrons in an atom share all four — caps every orbital at two electrons of
    opposite spin, and the filling order (aufbau) then builds the table automatically:
    shell capacities 2, 8, 18 emerge from 2n², and the row lengths (2, 8, 8, 18…) are the
    energy-level spacing of the Coulomb problem. Chemical families are configurations:
    the alkali metals all end in one lonely s¹ electron (hence violent reactivity), the
    noble gases end in a filled shell (hence near-inertness), and halogens need exactly
    one electron (hence their appetite). Mendeleev (1869) organised by observation; Schrödinger
    (1926) explained the organisation. One caution the quantum picture forces: an orbital
    is a probability distribution, not a path — the Bohr planet-orbit picture (1913) was
    scaffolding, demolished by de Broglie's matter waves and Heisenberg's uncertainty.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="stack the chain from wave equation to table" widget="order" config={qcChainOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "electrons orbit the nucleus like planets, and the
    orbitals are the paths." An orbital is a standing wave — a three-dimensional
    probability map where a measurement is likely to find the electron; there is no
    trajectory, and asking which path the electron took is physically meaningless
    (Heisenberg). The s orbital is a sphere of likelihood, the p orbitals two lobes —
    shapes of probability, not racetracks. Everything in this page inherits that correction.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — orbitals on trial" widget="judge" config={qcOrbitalJudge} />

  <h2>Isotopes: mass without (much) chemistry</h2>
  <p>
    Isotopes (2.3) — same element, different neutron count — expose the split between
    chemistry and nuclear physics: chemistry is set by Z (the electron count), so
    carbon-12 and carbon-14 behave almost identically in reactions, while their nuclei are
    utterly different (stable for one, 5,730-year half-life for the other). Mass
    spectrometry reads the difference: chlorine's relative atomic mass of 35.5 is the
    weighted average of 75.8% chlorine-35 and 24.2% chlorine-37 — no single chlorine atom
    weighs 35.5, a fact that only makes sense once isotopes exist. And the "almost
    identical" chemistry hides real quantum effects: the kinetic isotope effect makes
    O–H bonds react slightly differently from O–D bonds (zero-point energies differ), and
    heavy water D₂O boils at 101.4 °C rather than 100.0 °C — mass does matter, a little,
    through quantum zero-point motion. Isotope tracing (carbon-14 dating, deuterium
    labelling of metabolic pathways) turns that small difference into one of chemistry's
    most powerful experimental tools.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each fact — electron count or nucleus" widget="sort-buckets" config={qcIsotopeBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> electron configuration explains identity and the table's families;
    isotopes show that mass is a nuclear matter, almost irrelevant to chemistry. Now we put the
    electrons to work: what actually drives one atom to hand another an electron?
  </p>

  <h2>Ionic bonds: the energy ledger of electron transfer</h2>
  <p>
    Ionic bonding (2.4) is often taught as "atoms want full shells" — a sentence that
    survives no arithmetic. Sodium gives an electron to chlorine because the ledger says
    so, and the ledger is the Born–Haber cycle. For NaCl (values in kJ per mole): vaporise
    sodium (+108), half-dissociate chlorine (+122), ionise sodium (+496), attach the
    electron to chlorine (−349) — the sum so far is +377, strongly unfavourable. The
    lattice enthalpy closes it: assembling the ions into the crystal releases about −787,
    so the total (ΔH of formation) is ≈ −411 kJ/mol — favourable by a wide margin.
    The point is general and important: <strong>electron transfer is driven by electrostatic
    bookkeeping, not by shells "wanting"</strong>. The same ledger explains why MgCl₂ has
    the formula it does (two chlorines per magnesium balances charge and lattice), why
    ionic compounds have high melting points (you must pay the lattice enthalpy back to
    melt them — NaCl's is 801 °C), and why they conduct only when molten or dissolved
    (mobile ions are the charge carriers, not electrons).
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="one line of the Born–Haber ledger is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={qcLedgerSpot} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "ionic compounds conduct electricity." Solid ionic
    compounds conduct essentially not at all: the ions are locked in the lattice, so the
    charge carriers cannot move — a salt crystal is an insulator, or the sea would short
    every coastline. Conductivity appears only when the lattice is disrupted: molten
    (above 801 °C for NaCl) or dissolved, when the mobile ions can migrate and carry
    current between the electrodes. This is also why electrolysis of solid salt does
    nothing while electrolysis of brine is an industry.
  </p>

  <h2>Covalent bonds: sharing as standing waves</h2>
  <p>
    Covalent bonding (2.5) is the quantum mechanics: when two hydrogen atoms approach, the
    two 1s atomic orbitals combine into a bonding molecular orbital (electron density
    concentrated between the nuclei — a standing wave wrapped around both) and an
    antibonding one (a node between them). Two electrons fill the bonding orbital, and the
    energy drops: the H–H bond's length is 74 pm and its strength 436 kJ/mol, both
    measured and both computed from first principles. Bond order — (bonding electrons
    minus antibonding electrons) ÷ 2 — predicts stability and explains facts Lewis
    structures cannot: why O₂ is paramagnetic (its two highest electrons occupy separate
    antibonding orbitals with parallel spins — two lonely unpaired electrons, seen by
    liquid oxygen clinging to a magnet). Hybridisation reshapes the atomic orbitals to
    match the geometry organic chemistry needs: sp³ for tetrahedral methane (109.5°),
    sp² for flat ethene with its exposed π bond (the site of all addition chemistry),
    sp for linear ethyne. The valence-shell electron-pair repulsion (VSEPR) rules students
    memorise are the shadow cast by these orbital geometries.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each hybrid with its geometry" widget="match" config={qcHybridMatch} />

  <h2>Giant covalent structures: networks and their consequences</h2>
  <p>
    Giant covalent structures (2.6) — diamond, graphite, quartz — are covalent bonds
    continued forever, and the network's topology decides the material. Diamond: every
    carbon sp³-bonded to four neighbours in a tetrahedral lattice — hard (no plane of
    weakness), a very high melting point (sublimes near 3,600 °C), and surprisingly a
    superb thermal conductor (~2,000 W/(m·K), several times copper, carried by stiff-bond
    lattice vibrations) but an electrical insulator (band gap 5.5 eV — no accessible
    states). Graphite: the same atoms, sp²-bonded into sheets — the delocalised π system
    conducts along the layers (pencils write and graphite electrodes carry current),
    sheets slide over each other (lubricant), and the inter-layer bonds are so weak that
    Scotch tape peeling them off produced graphene (2004, Nobel 2010). Same element, same
    bonds-in-kind, different network: allotropy as architecture. Quartz (SiO₂) extends the
    lesson to mixed networks — each silicon shares oxygens four ways, giving rigidity and
    the piezoelectric behaviour that once kept every watch and radio oscillating.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the magnitude — diamond's exit temperature" widget="estimate" config={qcDiamondEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> ionic bonding is electrostatic bookkeeping and covalent bonding is
    orbital overlap — two regimes of one energy-minimisation principle. Giant structures show
    what happens when overlap never stops, and the topology of the network decides the material.
    The last bonding regime, metals, is the case localised bonds give up on entirely.
  </p>

  <h2>Metallic bonding: the electron sea as a Fermi gas</h2>
  <p>
    Metals (2.7) are the case covalent theory handles by giving up: too few valence
    electrons for localised bonds, so the electrons delocalise over the whole crystal —
    the "sea of electrons", formalised in quantum physics as a <strong>Fermi gas</strong>
    filling a band of levels up to the Fermi energy. Every metallic property follows.
    Conduction: the highest electrons sit just under empty states, so any field accelerates
    them — copper's single 4s electron per atom is the free rider that wires your house.
    Malleability: the bonding is non-directional, so planes of atoms can slip without
    breaking the glue (compare ionic crystals, where sliding brings like charges together
    and the lattice shatters — the reason salt crystals cleave and copper sheets bend).
    Lustre and thermal conduction: the mobile electron sea responds to light and carries
    kinetic energy. Alloying (steel, brass, solder) works by disrupting the slip planes —
    metallurgy as defect engineering. The band-structure continuation of this picture is
    the solid-state physics page's entire subject: metals, semiconductors and insulators
    are one theory with three occupancies.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="fill the blanks — the electron sea" widget="cloze" config={qcSeaCloze} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the octet rule explains bonding — atoms bond to reach
    eight outer electrons." Atoms have no wants; bonding happens when the total energy of
    the arrangement is lower than the separated atoms (that is the whole content of the
    Born–Haber ledger and of every molecular orbital diagram). The octet pattern is a
    useful summary of <em>when</em> low-energy arrangements occur for light main-group
    elements — and it fails constantly elsewhere: SF₆ has twelve electrons around sulfur,
    BF₃ is stable with six around boron, and transition metals ignore it by default.
    Energy is the explanation; octets are the mnemonic.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the four quantum numbers and state what each fixes about an electron's situation.</li>
    <li>In the Born–Haber cycle for NaCl, the steps before lattice assembly sum to roughly +377 kJ/mol. Where does the +787 kJ/mol of lattice enthalpy come from, and why does it dwarf the ionisation cost?</li>
    <li>Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon with covalent bonds. Why does one conduct electricity and the other not?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) n fixes shell size and energy; ℓ fixes orbital shape (s, p,
    d, f); mℓ fixes orientation; ms fixes spin. Pauli then caps every orbital at two electrons
    of opposite spin, and shell capacities follow. (2) Lattice enthalpy is released by
    assembling Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions into the crystal: the Coulomb attraction between opposite
    charges, summed over billions of ion pairs, pays for vaporising sodium, dissociating
    chlorine and ionising sodium several times over — which is exactly why the "atoms want full
    shells" story fails and electrostatic bookkeeping succeeds. (3) Diamond is sp³-bonded into a
    tetrahedral network with a 5.5 eV band gap — no accessible states, so an insulator.
    Graphite is sp²-bonded into sheets whose delocalised π system conducts along the layers.
    Same element, same bonds-in-kind, different topology — allotropy as architecture.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Schrödinger equation's standing-wave solutions are orbitals — probability maps, not paths; Bohr's orbits were scaffolding.</li>
    <li>Four quantum numbers address each electron; Pauli exclusion caps orbitals at two, and the filling order builds the periodic table automatically.</li>
    <li>Elements have unique line spectra; compounds are new quantum objects with levels belonging to neither constituent atom.</li>
    <li>Ionic bonding is Born–Haber bookkeeping — lattice enthalpy pays for electron transfer, not shell-filling desires.</li>
    <li>Covalent bonding is orbital overlap; bond order, hybridisation and VSEPR geometries are its consequences.</li>
    <li>Giant-structure topology decides materials: diamond insulates, graphite conducts, same element.</li>
    <li>Metallic bonding is a delocalised Fermi gas — conduction, malleability and lustre all follow from it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The orbital picture is chemistry's load-bearing beam, and the sibling expert lessons carry it
    further: <em>Polymer chemistry</em> shows what happens when covalent chains grow to tens of
    thousands of units, and <em>Biochemistry bridge</em> runs the same quantum rules under
    life's constraints — water-based, room-temperature, precise.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Computation replaced the slide rule.</strong> Exact solutions stop at hydrogen;
    helium (two electrons, three bodies) is already analytically hopeless, so quantum
    chemistry is the art of systematic approximation. Hartree–Fock theory treats each
    electron as moving in the average field of the rest; post-Hartree–Fock methods add the
    correlated motion back at rising cost; density functional theory (DFT) — Hohenberg,
    Kohn and Sham, Nobel 1998 (chemistry, shared with Pople) — swaps the intractable
    many-electron wavefunction for the electron density, a function of just three
    variables, at a quality-to-cost ratio that made it chemistry's workhorse. Today DFT
    screens battery cathodes, catalysts and drug candidates before any synthesis; the 2013
    Nobel-winning methods for simulating proteins on classical computers and the coming
    quantum computers (Feynman's 1982 suggestion that only quantum machines can
    efficiently simulate quantum systems) are the same programme accelerating.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Relativistic chemistry.</strong> For heavy elements the electrons move fast
    enough that special relativity bends the periodic table itself: inner electrons
    contract, outer s orbitals stabilise, and anomalies appear — gold's colour (relativistic
    shifts absorb blue light; silver, one row up, stays white), mercury's liquidity (the
    relativistically stabilised 6s electrons bond poorly), and lead-acid chemistry's
    inert pair effect. Uncertain claims are rare here because the computations and spectra
    agree: relativistic quantum chemistry is a solved-in-principle field that quietly
    explains a shelf of "weird element" facts. The periodic table, it turns out, is not
    just Schrödinger's equation — it is Schrödinger's equation plus the speed of light.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open frontiers.</strong> The electron correlation problem is exact only for
    few-electron systems; density functionals remain calibrated approximations whose
    failures (self-interaction error, dispersion forces) are active research. Excited
    states, photochemistry and catalytic transition states strain current methods. And
    the grand computational goal — first-principles prediction of a material's synthesis
    route, not just its properties — remains open. Meanwhile the conceptual spine of this
    page is settled physics and will not move: quantised levels, Pauli exclusion, orbital
    overlap, energy minimisation. Every new result in the field is a more precise
    description of those four things doing more complicated dances.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Schrödinger (1926); Pauling, The Nature of the Chemical Bond (1939); Pople & Kohn, Nobel lectures (1998). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
